## Target Expression Tagging

- In text intended to display or highlight the lexical target, wrap each visible target surface with `<t inf="LABEL">surface</t>`.
- Tag only occurrences of the intended lexical target, not unrelated words or coincidental matches.
- Preserve the actual surface form and select its corresponding inflection label:
  - `base`: base or invariant form.
  - `past`: simple past.
  - `past_participle`: past participle.
  - `present_3sg`: third-person singular present.
  - `ing`: -ing form.
  - `plural`: plural noun.
  - `comparative`: comparative form.
  - `superlative`: superlative form.
- Tag each fixed component of a multi-word expression separately, even when separated by other words. Leave variable arguments and intervening text outside the tags.
- Do not introduce the target solely to satisfy tagging. If the target is intentionally hidden (e.g., a cloze blank), do not tag the placeholder or reveal the missing expression.

Example for "bring up":

"She <t inf="past">brought</t> the issue <t inf="base">up</t> during the meeting."
